Urticaria (Hives)
Dermatology & Soft Tissue
Intensely pruritic wheals, Transitory/migratory lesions <24 hrs, Central pallor with red flare

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DST-046
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe, rapidly spreading, intensely pruritic body rash causing extreme distress and fear of anaphylaxis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, Partner
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage documented an acute onset of a rapidly spreading, intensely itchy rash covering the patient's entire body, leading to severe panic about a potential life-threatening systemic allergic reaction. Urgent assessment was required to definitively rule out impending anaphylaxis (airway compromise, hypotension) and to provide immediate, high-dose antihistamine therapy to break the severe itch cycle before the next available routine appointment.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I'm covered head-to-toe in these huge, itchy welts that keep moving around my body. I feel like my skin is burning and I'm terrified my throat is going to close."
History of Present Illness: The patient was in their usual state of health until 6 hours ago when they noticed a sudden eruption of intensely pruritic erythematous wheals on the abdomen. Over the past few hours, the lesions have demonstrated a rapid, migratory pattern, appearing and disappearing across the trunk and extremities. The patient notes the transitory nature of the rash, with individual lesions resolving within a few hours while new ones erupt elsewhere. The lesions vary from small papules to large confluent polycyclic plaques. The patient denies shortness of breath, lip or tongue swelling, vomiting, or dizziness. They report the severe itching is significantly worsening at night and exacerbated by scratching.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently highly agitated due to severe pruritus.
Social/Safety Concerns: Extreme anxiety regarding a potential anaphylactic reaction.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/82 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Visibly distressed, continuously scratching and rubbing the arms and legs, but speaking in full sentences without respiratory distress.
Physical Exam: The trunk and bilateral extremities exhibit multiple, widespread, intensely pruritic wheals. The lesions present with distinct central pallor surrounded by a prominent red flare. Firm pressure applied to the erythematous areas produces complete blanching. The sizes of the plaques are highly variable, with some measuring up to 10 cm in diameter. There is a complete absence of surface scaling, blistering, or excoriation (due to rubbing rather than deep scratching). Oropharyngeal examination reveals a clear airway, no uvular edema, and normal breath sounds. No associated deep tissue angioedema of the face, lips, or eyelids is present.
Environmental Observations: Clean environment.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient took a new brand of ibuprofen 12 hours ago.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Urticaria (Hives) - Status: Acute, high symptomatic burden
Differentials:

Anaphylaxis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal blood pressure, absence of wheeze or stridor, no gastrointestinal symptoms, and isolated cutaneous involvement.

Erythema Multiforme - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The transitory, migratory nature of the wheals (EM lesions are fixed for days) and the lack of true three-zone target lesions or mucosal involvement.

Contact Dermatitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The generalized, migratory distribution rather than a localized, fixed geometric pattern corresponding to a specific exposure, and the absence of scaling or vesicles.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low (benign cutaneous condition, high anxiety)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, highly distressing eruption of generalized urticaria. Triage correctly prioritized the rapid spread of the rash as a potential precursor to anaphylaxis. Immediate out-of-hours clinical assessment was vital to physically examine the airway, assess hemodynamic stability, and definitively rule out a life-threatening systemic allergic reaction. By confirming that the reaction is entirely confined to the superficial dermis, the intervention successfully de-escalated the patient's severe panic. Providing an immediate stat dose of a potent oral antihistamine rapidly reduced the debilitating pruritus, averting an unnecessary emergency department presentation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral Cetirizine 20mg stat. Prescribed a high-dose, non-sedating antihistamine regimen (Cetirizine 20mg BD for up to 14 days) to suppress the histamine release. Advised immediate cessation of all NSAIDs (e.g., ibuprofen) as a likely trigger.
Positive Behavioral Change: By taking the antihistamine continuously every 12 hours, even if your skin looks temporarily clear, you will keep your body's histamine levels suppressed and stop the hives from immediately returning when the medication wears off. By applying cool, damp washcloths to your skin instead of scratching, you will physically calm the irritated nerve endings and instantly reduce the severe burning itch.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not required.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not required.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3-5 days to ensure the urticaria is resolving and discuss potential triggers if it becomes chronic.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If you develop any swelling around your lips, tongue, or throat, if you suddenly feel faint, dizzy, or start vomiting, or if you experience any difficulty breathing or wheezing, call 000 immediately for an ambulance.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Urticaria is highly unpredictable. While currently confined to the skin, severe allergic reactions can sometimes evolve rapidly.
Clinical Course: The intense itching and raised welts will begin to subside within 1-2 hours of the antihistamine. However, the rash will likely continue to come and go in waves for up to 2-6 weeks.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Monitor your breathing closely. The skin rash itself is completely harmless, but any internal swelling is a medical emergency.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A trigger (likely the new medication) caused immune cells in your skin to dump histamine, creating these migrating, itchy fluid pockets. The high-dose antihistamines act as a direct block to this chemical, stopping the itch and the swelling.
Actionable Steps: Do not take ibuprofen, aspirin, or any NSAID medications. Wear loose cotton clothing and take cool showers to minimize skin heating.

